Try, Throw & Catch
- a way to handle errors without breaking the program's structure
- seperates normal logic from error logic (cleaner code, error handling is somewhere else -> no interrupting return value)
- however do not use when expected behavior (like checking if a file exists) (only for when this should not happen in normal execution)
- ex. if (something_wrong)
        return -1;
    INSTEAD 
      if (something_wrong) 
        throw MyException();
Examples of each
- try (defines the code to test) (ex. if division == 0) (ex. if age == 15)
- throw (triggers an exception) (ex. throw 505 error code) (ex. throw (age))
- catch (handles the error) (ex. input error code, and prints "error occured with 505") (ex. "access denied - must be 18. age is xyage")




Exception class
define: is a special class used to represent an error
to use an exception class: 
- first, define the class by derividing it from the standard exception class std::exception
- at least one constructor (takes one or more arguments that describes details of the exception and other methods)

- to throw an exception, use "throw" followed by an instance of the exception class


- without std::exception id have to catch every error type seperatly
- they derive from std::exception so that all my exceptions share a common interface & can be caught generically
- having Exceptioni class inherit from std::exception allows the catch block to catch any derived exception



what throw does
if (grade > 150)
    throw GradeTooLowException();
1. creates an exception object
2. stops execution of the current function
3. jumps to the nearest matching catch 



the throw has the message but only catch decides what to do with it like printing.
the catch block is the only place you should actually print

virtual const char* what() const noexcept;
- a virtual function defined in std::exception
- returns a c-style string (const char*)
- string is the error message describing the exception
- noexcept just means it guarantees it wont throw another exception